1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,394
Two complaints, and no bug in GitHub. CI
is the automated testing that runs on each

2
00:00:09,434 --> 00:00:12,783
change, and a dependency is a
package your project needs.

3
00:00:12,823 --> 00:00:17,241
The first complaint is a cache doing
exactly what a cache is defined to do.

4
00:00:17,281 --> 00:00:22,057
The second is a team that rebuilt in the
deploy job what it had already built and

5
00:00:22,097 --> 00:00:26,575
tested in another job. They got a
different result, because the dependencies

6
00:00:26,615 --> 00:00:27,467
resolved again.

7
00:00:27,807 --> 00:00:32,761
Both come from using a cache or an artifact
without knowing what each one promises.

8
00:00:32,801 --> 00:00:37,755
A cache promises nothing: it may be absent.
An artifact promises that the bytes you

9
00:00:37,795 --> 00:00:42,629
download are the bytes that were uploaded.
This video gives you both definitions,

10
00:00:42,669 --> 00:00:47,683
and the rules for ordering and multiplying
jobs that you need before either matters.

11
00:00:47,723 --> 00:00:52,437
Keep the first complaint in mind. Why
would CI keep installing the old version?

12
00:00:54,277 --> 00:00:57,903
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:57,943 --> 00:01:02,651
In GitHub Actions, a workflow is a file
that says: when this event happens, run

14
00:01:02,691 --> 00:01:07,459
these jobs. A job is a list of steps that
run in order on one fresh machine, the

15
00:01:07,499 --> 00:01:12,328
runner. You can now read a trigger, an
expression and a shell invocation, and you

16
00:01:12,368 --> 00:01:17,376
know what the checkout leaves on the runner.
This video is about the structure above

17
00:01:17,416 --> 00:01:19,841
the steps: how jobs relate to each other.

18
00:01:20,181 --> 00:01:23,767
Four topics, in the order of
sections 20A.9 to 20A.12.

19
00:01:23,807 --> 00:01:29,208
Controlling jobs with needs, if, timeout-minutes
and continue-on-error, including

20
00:01:29,248 --> 00:01:33,909
two rules about skipping that decide
whether a pull request can merge.

21
00:01:33,949 --> 00:01:38,543
A pull request is GitHub's proposal
to merge one branch into another.

22
00:01:38,583 --> 00:01:42,371
Matrix strategies, and how to
count the jobs of a matrix.

23
00:01:42,411 --> 00:01:46,871
Dependency caching: keys, restore
order, scope, eviction and trust.

24
00:01:46,911 --> 00:01:51,035
And artifacts: what crosses from
one job to the next as files.

25
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:56,945
The local demonstration computes a cache
key and consumes a simulated artifact.

26
00:01:56,985 --> 00:01:59,714
The matrix is yours to run in Lab 26.8.

27
00:02:01,554 --> 00:02:03,132
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:02:03,472 --> 00:02:04,885
After this video you can:

29
00:02:05,225 --> 00:02:09,488
order jobs with needs and predict what if,
timeout-minutes and continue-on-error do

30
00:02:09,528 --> 00:02:10,525
to the run's result;

31
00:02:10,865 --> 00:02:13,846
count the jobs of a matrix
with include and exclude;

32
00:02:14,186 --> 00:02:17,930
design a cache key and say what
happens on a miss and on a stale hit;

33
00:02:18,270 --> 00:02:22,985
distinguish a cache from an artifact
by purpose, scope, lifetime and trust;

34
00:02:23,325 --> 00:02:26,367
carry one build from a
build job to a later job.

35
00:02:28,207 --> 00:02:33,206
Controlling jobs. In one sentence: needs
orders jobs and carries their results, if

36
00:02:33,246 --> 00:02:38,307
decides whether a job or step runs,
timeout-minutes bounds how long it may run, and

37
00:02:38,347 --> 00:02:42,671
continue-on-error lets a failure pass
without failing what contains it.

38
00:02:43,011 --> 00:02:46,696
needs, at job level: the job
waits for the listed jobs.

39
00:02:46,736 --> 00:02:51,775
If one of them fails or is skipped,
this job is skipped, unless its if says

40
00:02:51,815 --> 00:02:54,959
otherwise. Without needs,
jobs run in parallel.

41
00:02:55,298 --> 00:03:00,053
if, at job or step level: the job or
step runs when the expression is truthy,

42
00:03:00,093 --> 00:03:05,158
meaning it counts as true. An expression
is a formula that GitHub Actions replaces

43
00:03:05,198 --> 00:03:09,953
with its value. An if without a status
function behaves as if success() and a

44
00:03:09,993 --> 00:03:13,875
logical "and" were in front of
it. So the default is success().

45
00:03:14,215 --> 00:03:19,124
timeout-minutes: the job or step is
cancelled after that many minutes.

46
00:03:19,164 --> 00:03:21,175
The default for a job is 360.

47
00:03:21,515 --> 00:03:26,895
continue-on-error: a failure doesn't fail
the job, at step level, or the run, at job

48
00:03:26,935 --> 00:03:27,283
level.

49
00:03:27,623 --> 00:03:30,505
The status functions change
the implicit success().

50
00:03:30,545 --> 00:03:33,943
failure() is true when an earlier
step or needed job failed.

51
00:03:33,983 --> 00:03:38,069
always() is true even when the run
was cancelled. cancelled() is true on

52
00:03:38,109 --> 00:03:42,825
cancellation. The expressions page advises
against always() for anything that could

53
00:03:42,865 --> 00:03:47,352
fail critically and recommends !cancelled()
for "run whether or not the earlier

54
00:03:47,392 --> 00:03:50,447
steps passed". You saw that
form in the lint workflow.

55
00:03:50,787 --> 00:03:54,285
continue-on-error at step level
splits a step's result in two.

56
00:03:54,325 --> 00:03:57,480
The outcome is the result
before the setting is applied.

57
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:01,932
The conclusion is the result after. A
failed step with the setting has outcome

58
00:04:01,972 --> 00:04:03,701
failure and conclusion success.

59
00:04:04,041 --> 00:04:09,275
Quick quiz. A required check is a check
that a rule says must pass before a pull

60
00:04:09,315 --> 00:04:13,429
request can merge. Suppose that
job gets skipped by its own if.

61
00:04:13,469 --> 00:04:17,649
Does the check A, block the pull
request, or B, count as passed?

62
00:04:17,689 --> 00:04:18,440
Your answer?

63
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:27,714
B, and most people say A the first time.
There are two rules about skipping.

64
00:04:27,754 --> 00:04:32,658
They decide whether a pull request can merge,
and they point in opposite directions.

65
00:04:32,998 --> 00:04:36,306
A job skipped by its if
reports success for its check.

66
00:04:36,346 --> 00:04:38,352
So a required check is satisfied.

67
00:04:38,692 --> 00:04:43,150
A workflow that never starts, because
of a path or branch filter or a skip

68
00:04:43,190 --> 00:04:47,040
instruction, reports nothing. So
a required check stays pending.

69
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:52,397
The textbook's summary: they make if on a
job a way to bypass a required check by

70
00:04:52,437 --> 00:04:57,266
accident, and paths on a workflow a way
to block every unrelated pull request.

71
00:04:57,606 --> 00:05:02,170
And one instruction for production:
set timeout-minutes on every job.

72
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:07,774
The default of 360 minutes is also the hard
limit for a GitHub-hosted job, so a test

73
00:05:07,814 --> 00:05:12,445
that hangs on a network call bills
six hours unless you say otherwise.

74
00:05:12,485 --> 00:05:15,180
The course workflows use 5 to 20 minutes.

75
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:20,558
Matrix. In one sentence: a matrix turns
one job definition into one job per

76
00:05:20,598 --> 00:05:25,907
combination of the values you list, each
with its own runner and its own matrix

77
00:05:25,947 --> 00:05:26,449
context.

78
00:05:26,789 --> 00:05:32,130
strategy.matrix maps variable names to
lists, and the jobs are the Cartesian

79
00:05:32,170 --> 00:05:36,024
product: every combination
of one value from each list.

80
00:05:36,064 --> 00:05:36,378
Then:

81
00:05:36,718 --> 00:05:40,116
exclude removes combinations,
and a partial match is enough.

82
00:05:40,456 --> 00:05:45,454
include is processed after exclude. An entry
is added to every existing combination

83
00:05:45,494 --> 00:05:49,034
it can extend without overwriting
an original matrix value.

84
00:05:49,074 --> 00:05:53,101
If it would overwrite one, it becomes
a new combination of its own.

85
00:05:53,441 --> 00:05:58,869
fail-fast defaults to true: when one matrix
job fails, the in-progress and queued

86
00:05:58,909 --> 00:06:02,852
ones are cancelled. max-parallel
caps how many run at once.

87
00:06:03,192 --> 00:06:07,251
A run may generate at
most 256 matrix jobs.

88
00:06:07,591 --> 00:06:12,715
A job-level if is evaluated before the
matrix is expanded, so it can't read matrix.

89
00:06:13,055 --> 00:06:18,099
And outputs of matrix jobs are merged into
one set, and the last writer wins, in no

90
00:06:18,139 --> 00:06:19,140
guaranteed order.

91
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:24,168
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds.
Workflow 10 lists two operating systems and

92
00:06:24,208 --> 00:06:29,070
three Python versions. It excludes one
combination, and it includes one entry with a

93
00:06:29,110 --> 00:06:32,338
fourth Python version. Draw
the grid and count the jobs.

94
00:06:34,678 --> 00:06:39,295
Six. Two times three is six cells.
One is excluded, which leaves five.

95
00:06:39,335 --> 00:06:44,152
The included entry would overwrite an
original value, so it becomes a new

96
00:06:44,192 --> 00:06:46,413
combination of its own: six again.

97
00:06:46,754 --> 00:06:51,964
One thing is unverified: the names of the
checks that matrix jobs report aren't

98
00:06:52,004 --> 00:06:55,886
specified on any official page
the course's research found.

99
00:06:55,926 --> 00:07:01,469
So workflow 10 sets the job name from the
matrix values, and adds a final job with a

100
00:07:01,509 --> 00:07:02,200
fixed name.

101
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:07,563
Caching. In one sentence: a cache stores a
directory under a key so that a later run

102
00:07:07,603 --> 00:07:10,879
with the same key can restore
it and skip the download.

103
00:07:10,919 --> 00:07:15,821
It's an optimization that may be absent,
never a place to keep something you need.

104
00:07:16,161 --> 00:07:20,706
The key: at most 512 characters, usually
built from the operating system and

105
00:07:20,746 --> 00:07:25,774
hashFiles of a lock file. A lock file pins
each dependency to one exact version, and

106
00:07:25,814 --> 00:07:30,841
a hash is a fingerprint computed from content.
An existing key is never overwritten.

107
00:07:30,881 --> 00:07:35,245
The documentation: "You cannot change
the contents of an existing cache."

108
00:07:35,585 --> 00:07:40,858
The restore order. First the exact key.
Then entries whose key starts with the key.

109
00:07:40,898 --> 00:07:45,979
Then each restore-keys prefix in order,
the most recently created match winning.

110
00:07:46,319 --> 00:07:51,366
Saving: on a miss, the cache is saved at
the end of the job, and only if the job

111
00:07:51,406 --> 00:07:52,828
completes successfully.

112
00:07:53,168 --> 00:07:57,225
The cache-hit output: true only for
an exact match on the primary key.

113
00:07:57,265 --> 00:08:00,619
False when a restore key matched,
or nothing was restored.

114
00:08:00,959 --> 00:08:06,139
Scope: a run can restore caches created on
its own branch or on the default branch.

115
00:08:06,179 --> 00:08:09,725
A pull request run can also
restore from its base branch.

116
00:08:09,765 --> 00:08:15,008
Sibling branches can't read each other's
caches. A cache saved by a pull request run

117
00:08:15,048 --> 00:08:19,662
belongs to the merge ref, GitHub's test
merge of that pull request, and is

118
00:08:19,702 --> 00:08:22,681
restorable only by re-runs
of that pull request.

119
00:08:23,021 --> 00:08:26,685
Eviction: entries not accessed
for seven days are removed.

120
00:08:26,725 --> 00:08:31,921
A repository gets ten gigabytes without
charge. Above the limit the least recently

121
00:08:31,961 --> 00:08:36,966
used entries are deleted. Administrators
can raise the limit, billed, since the

122
00:08:37,006 --> 00:08:38,690
twentieth of November 2025.

123
00:08:39,030 --> 00:08:43,964
Trust: anyone who can open a pull request
can read caches of the base branch, and

124
00:08:44,004 --> 00:08:48,752
cache contents aren't signed. Since the
twenty-sixth of June 2026, events that

125
00:08:48,792 --> 00:08:53,910
people without write access can cause get
a read-only token for the default branch's

126
00:08:53,950 --> 00:08:58,944
caches. And since the tenth of September
2026 the cache-mode key states the access

127
00:08:58,984 --> 00:09:02,689
explicitly, with the values read,
write, write-only and none.

128
00:09:02,729 --> 00:09:04,961
Cache poisoning is a topic of Part 7.

129
00:09:05,301 --> 00:09:10,719
Most projects never write a cache block,
because the setup actions, packaged steps,

130
00:09:10,759 --> 00:09:16,243
contain it. actions/setup-java with cache:
maven stores the Maven repository under a

131
00:09:16,283 --> 00:09:21,635
key that hashes the POM files by default.
astral-sh/setup-uv has enable-cache with

132
00:09:21,675 --> 00:09:26,895
the default auto: enabled on GitHub-hosted
runners except for release, tag push,

133
00:09:26,935 --> 00:09:29,789
pull_request_target and
workflow_run events.

134
00:09:30,129 --> 00:09:35,159
Artifacts. In one sentence: an artifact
is a set of files that a job uploads to

135
00:09:35,199 --> 00:09:40,100
GitHub, where it's stored with the run,
can be downloaded by later jobs or by

136
00:09:40,140 --> 00:09:42,988
people, and expires after
a retention period.

137
00:09:43,328 --> 00:09:46,815
Immutability: an artifact
can't be changed after upload.

138
00:09:46,855 --> 00:09:51,664
A second upload with the same name in
one run fails unless overwrite is true.

139
00:09:52,004 --> 00:09:57,570
Availability: downloadable by a later job
of the same run as soon as the upload step

140
00:09:57,610 --> 00:09:57,903
ends.

141
00:09:58,243 --> 00:10:03,519
Retention: 90 days by default. retention-days
goes from 1 to 90, never above the

142
00:10:03,559 --> 00:10:09,100
repository or organization setting. Since
the first of October 2026 the same setting

143
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:12,754
also deletes checks, workflow
runs and commit statuses.

144
00:10:13,094 --> 00:10:18,931
Limits: 500 artifacts per job. Hidden
files are excluded unless you include them

145
00:10:18,971 --> 00:10:19,739
explicitly.

146
00:10:20,079 --> 00:10:23,477
Permissions: zipped uploads
don't keep file permissions.

147
00:10:23,517 --> 00:10:26,607
If the executable bit
matters, tar the files first.

148
00:10:26,947 --> 00:10:33,547
Outputs: an artifact ID, a URL, and a SHA-256
digest, a fingerprint of the uploaded

149
00:10:33,587 --> 00:10:34,027
bytes.

150
00:10:34,367 --> 00:10:40,023
Download: by name into a path. Version 8
fails on a digest mismatch by default.

151
00:10:41,863 --> 00:10:42,836
A picture helps.

152
00:10:43,176 --> 00:10:47,828
Analogy, from the textbook. A cache is
the shared fridge at work with labelled

153
00:10:47,868 --> 00:10:51,256
boxes. If a box with your label
is there, lunch is quick.

154
00:10:51,296 --> 00:10:56,189
If it was cleared out, you cook again. You
don't keep your passport in it, and you

155
00:10:56,229 --> 00:10:59,196
don't eat from a box whose
label you didn't write.

156
00:10:59,536 --> 00:11:02,746
The textbook says where it
holds and where it breaks.

157
00:11:02,786 --> 00:11:07,835
It holds for eviction and for trust. It
breaks on mutability, because a cache entry

158
00:11:07,875 --> 00:11:11,760
can never be changed once saved.
The fridge box can be refilled.

159
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:12,864
A cache key can't.

160
00:11:13,204 --> 00:11:17,795
That immutability is the mechanism behind
the first complaint from the opening.

161
00:11:17,835 --> 00:11:22,426
If the key doesn't change when the
dependencies change, the old box keeps being

162
00:11:22,466 --> 00:11:22,777
found.

163
00:11:23,117 --> 00:11:27,957
Then the documentation's rule for choosing
between the two: a cache for files that

164
00:11:27,997 --> 00:11:31,943
are reused between runs and can be
recreated, such as dependencies.

165
00:11:31,983 --> 00:11:36,644
An artifact for files a job produced that
must be passed to another job or kept

166
00:11:36,684 --> 00:11:39,857
after the run, such as build
outputs and test reports.

167
00:11:40,197 --> 00:11:44,784
And the reason behind that rule, which
the textbook sets in bold: the files that

168
00:11:44,824 --> 00:11:49,353
were tested are the files that are deployed.
A rebuild in a second job resolves

169
00:11:49,393 --> 00:11:52,302
dependencies again, possibly
to different versions.

170
00:11:52,642 --> 00:11:57,328
So when you see data moving between jobs,
ask: is this something I could lose

171
00:11:57,368 --> 00:12:01,317
without consequence? Then it's a
cache. Is this the thing itself?

172
00:12:01,357 --> 00:12:02,667
Then it's an artifact.

173
00:12:04,507 --> 00:12:08,958
Check your paper against the grid. Six
cells, one crossed out, and one new

174
00:12:08,998 --> 00:12:12,903
combination outside the grid: six
jobs, one of them experimental.

175
00:12:13,243 --> 00:12:17,275
And outside the grid, the last job
of the workflow: "All matrix tests".

176
00:12:17,615 --> 00:12:22,154
It needs test, and it has if: always().
That one fixed name is what a ruleset

177
00:12:22,194 --> 00:12:22,689
requires.

178
00:12:23,029 --> 00:12:27,692
Read the root-cause line: caches are
immutable per key, and restore keys match by

179
00:12:27,732 --> 00:12:28,098
prefix.

180
00:12:29,938 --> 00:12:30,698
Into the lab.

181
00:12:31,038 --> 00:12:33,271
Step 1: a key from the lock file.

182
00:12:33,611 --> 00:12:38,689
A small lock file with one package at one
version, and the first sixteen characters

183
00:12:38,729 --> 00:12:39,429
of its hash.

184
00:12:39,769 --> 00:12:41,364
Step 2: two commits.

185
00:12:41,704 --> 00:12:46,418
git commit adds an object and moves the
branch. The first commit changes only the

186
00:12:46,458 --> 00:12:49,705
README. The second changes
the version in the lock file.

187
00:12:50,045 --> 00:12:52,731
You've seen the README
commit leave the hash alone.

188
00:12:52,771 --> 00:12:57,169
Now the lock file itself changes. What
happens to the hash, and what does that mean

189
00:12:57,209 --> 00:12:59,682
for the cache on the next
run? Say it out loud.

190
00:13:09,522 --> 00:13:13,416
After the README commit: the
same hash. Same key, cache hit.

191
00:13:13,756 --> 00:13:18,790
After the dependency update: a new hash.
New key, cache miss, the dependencies are

192
00:13:18,830 --> 00:13:23,244
downloaded, and a new cache is saved
after the job, if the job succeeds.

193
00:13:23,584 --> 00:13:28,052
Now turn it around. If the key had been
built without the lock file, both commits

194
00:13:28,092 --> 00:13:32,560
would have hit the old entry. That's the
stale hit of the root-cause box, and the

195
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:35,008
first complaint from
the opening, explained.

196
00:13:35,348 --> 00:13:41,247
Step 3: consuming an artifact. Replay
labs/run ch20a/lab-26-7-artifact.

197
00:13:41,287 --> 00:13:48,106
The sample project has a deployment script
that simulates a deployment and changes

198
00:13:48,146 --> 00:13:49,528
nothing anywhere.

199
00:13:49,868 --> 00:13:54,344
The two empty files stand in for the build
outputs that a download step would place

200
00:13:54,384 --> 00:13:54,780
in dist.

201
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:58,643
The script names the environment, the
target, the commit and ref, which are

202
00:13:58,683 --> 00:14:02,158
"unknown" and "local" outside a runner,
and the two files it would deploy.

203
00:14:02,198 --> 00:14:02,823
Exit status 0.

204
00:14:03,163 --> 00:14:05,621
Step 4: when the artifact did not arrive.

205
00:14:05,961 --> 00:14:09,061
An empty directory, then a
misspelled environment name.

206
00:14:09,101 --> 00:14:13,742
What should a deployment script do in each
case, and what would a careless one do?

207
00:14:13,782 --> 00:14:14,941
Make your prediction.

208
00:14:19,781 --> 00:14:24,465
Exit status 1 for "no build outputs", exit
status 2 for the unknown environment.

209
00:14:24,505 --> 00:14:29,307
The script refuses. A careless one would
report success on an empty directory, and

210
00:14:29,347 --> 00:14:34,030
the run would be green with nothing
deployed. Whatever consumes an artifact must

211
00:14:34,070 --> 00:14:35,329
check that it arrived.

212
00:14:40,669 --> 00:14:44,467
Read the test job. The name is
built from the matrix values.

213
00:14:44,507 --> 00:14:47,728
runs-on takes the operating
system from the matrix.

214
00:14:47,768 --> 00:14:52,909
continue-on-error takes its value from
matrix.experimental, so it's true only for

215
00:14:52,949 --> 00:14:58,154
one job. fail-fast is false, so one failing
combination doesn't cancel the others.

216
00:14:58,494 --> 00:15:03,546
Then the all-tests job. if: always() makes
it run even when a matrix job failed.

217
00:15:03,586 --> 00:15:08,511
Why is that necessary? Because a job that
needs a failed job is skipped, and a

218
00:15:08,551 --> 00:15:12,840
skipped job reports success, which
would satisfy the required check.

219
00:15:12,880 --> 00:15:17,933
With always() the job runs, and then fails
unless the result of test is success.

220
00:15:18,273 --> 00:15:23,393
Also unverified: how a needed matrix
job's result is aggregated when its only

221
00:15:23,433 --> 00:15:28,687
failure had continue-on-error. The
expectation is success, and Lab 26.8 has you

222
00:15:28,727 --> 00:15:29,424
observe it.

223
00:15:29,764 --> 00:15:33,785
Now Lab 26.8 on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

224
00:15:33,825 --> 00:15:39,134
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference,

225
00:15:39,174 --> 00:15:44,547
and no GitHub output was captured by the
authors. Before you open the run, write the

226
00:15:44,587 --> 00:15:49,767
list of jobs on paper: operating system,
Python version, experimental or not, and

227
00:15:49,807 --> 00:15:55,181
the name each will show. Then open the pull
request's checks and the run, and count.

228
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:57,823
Find the aggregate job by its fixed name.

229
00:15:59,663 --> 00:16:01,311
Five mistakes to watch for.

230
00:16:01,651 --> 00:16:06,106
A cache key without the lock file's
hash, or with a broad restore key.

231
00:16:06,146 --> 00:16:11,500
Root cause: caches are immutable per key
and restore keys match by prefix, so an old

232
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:13,233
entry keeps being restored.

233
00:16:13,573 --> 00:16:18,595
Rebuilding in the deploy job. Root cause:
a second build resolves dependencies

234
00:16:18,635 --> 00:16:23,463
again, so the deployed files are not
the tested files; pass the build as an

235
00:16:23,503 --> 00:16:24,047
artifact.

236
00:16:24,387 --> 00:16:29,437
An if on a job that is a required check.
Root cause: a job skipped by its if reports

237
00:16:29,477 --> 00:16:30,770
success for its check.

238
00:16:31,110 --> 00:16:36,677
No timeout-minutes. Root cause: the default
of 360 minutes is also the hard limit

239
00:16:36,717 --> 00:16:40,762
for a GitHub-hosted job, so a
hanging test bills six hours.

240
00:16:41,102 --> 00:16:46,282
Requiring the individual matrix jobs in a
ruleset. Root cause: their set and names

241
00:16:46,322 --> 00:16:51,121
change when the matrix changes; require
one aggregate job with a fixed name.

242
00:16:52,961 --> 00:16:57,566
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's
instruction for an ML team: cache package

243
00:16:57,606 --> 00:17:01,549
downloads and small test models,
never multi-gigabyte checkpoints.

244
00:17:01,889 --> 00:17:06,732
Here's how that goes wrong. A team that
evaluates language models caches a model

245
00:17:06,772 --> 00:17:10,883
checkpoint of several gigabytes "to
make the evaluation job faster".

246
00:17:10,923 --> 00:17:14,850
It works. A week later every other
job in the repository is slow.

247
00:17:14,890 --> 00:17:19,856
The ten gigabytes per repository are shared
by all branches, the checkpoint filled

248
00:17:19,896 --> 00:17:24,617
most of them, and above the limit the
least recently used entries are deleted.

249
00:17:24,657 --> 00:17:28,524
The model cache evicted the
dependency caches that made CI fast.

250
00:17:28,864 --> 00:17:33,560
The decision is the one from the mental
model. Can the checkpoint be lost without

251
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:38,297
consequence? It can be downloaded again,
so it's cache material in principle, but

252
00:17:38,337 --> 00:17:41,922
its size makes it the wrong tenant
for a shared ten gigabytes.

253
00:17:41,962 --> 00:17:46,366
And for upload of reports from that
evaluation, the textbook adds two rules.

254
00:17:46,706 --> 00:17:50,352
Upload test reports with if:
failure() and a short retention.

255
00:17:50,392 --> 00:17:55,427
And never upload a directory that may contain
credential files or a .git folder with

256
00:17:55,467 --> 00:18:00,321
persisted credentials, because an artifact
is a copy of those files that outlives

257
00:18:00,361 --> 00:18:00,804
the job.

258
00:18:02,644 --> 00:18:09,017
Your turn. Do Lab 26.4, "Workflow 4,
Python tests with uv and caching", in

259
00:18:09,057 --> 00:18:14,303
lab-manual/m26-actions-fundamentals.md,
in your normal shell.

260
00:18:14,643 --> 00:18:19,369
Before each run, predict whether the cache
will be hit or missed, and say which file

261
00:18:19,409 --> 00:18:22,546
decides. After a change to
the lock file, predict again.

262
00:18:22,886 --> 00:18:28,480
The challenge is Exercise 26.6,
"Caches and artifacts", in

263
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:32,172
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

264
00:18:36,012 --> 00:18:41,060
Answer out loud. A strong answer takes the
four words in the question as four rows

265
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:45,031
and fills both columns for each,
with the facts from this video.

266
00:18:45,071 --> 00:18:48,444
For trust, it says who can
read and who can write each.

267
00:18:48,484 --> 00:18:52,602
Then it answers the last sentence
with a reason, not only a choice.

268
00:18:52,642 --> 00:18:57,442
The follow-up is the first line of the
hook: a lock file upgraded and CI still

269
00:18:57,482 --> 00:19:02,282
installing the old version. Explain the
mechanism from immutability and prefix

270
00:19:02,322 --> 00:19:05,137
matching, and give the
fix and the prevention.

271
00:19:06,977 --> 00:19:07,904
Let's land this.

272
00:19:08,244 --> 00:19:09,904
You should now be able to say:

273
00:19:10,244 --> 00:19:14,115
needs orders jobs; a job that needs a
failed or skipped job is skipped unless its

274
00:19:14,155 --> 00:19:15,177
own if says otherwise.

275
00:19:15,517 --> 00:19:19,915
A job skipped by its if reports success;
a workflow that never starts reports

276
00:19:19,955 --> 00:19:20,376
nothing.

277
00:19:20,716 --> 00:19:26,109
A matrix is the Cartesian product, minus
exclude, then include, which extends a

278
00:19:26,149 --> 00:19:28,173
combination or adds a new one.

279
00:19:28,513 --> 00:19:32,963
A cache is an immutable, evictable speed-up
keyed by content; a stale key gives

280
00:19:33,003 --> 00:19:34,043
stale dependencies.

281
00:19:34,383 --> 00:19:39,419
An artifact is the output of a job, immutable
and kept for a retention period: build

282
00:19:39,459 --> 00:19:42,320
once, pass the artifact,
deploy what was tested.

283
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:52,217
Read sections 20A.9 to 20A.12 of Chapter
20A. Do Lab 26.8, "Workflow 10, matrix

284
00:19:52,257 --> 00:19:57,547
testing", in
lab-manual/m26-actions-fundamentals.md.

285
00:19:57,887 --> 00:20:03,520
You can now say what a cache promises,
nothing, and what an artifact promises, the

286
00:20:03,560 --> 00:20:07,187
same bytes. Run Lab 26.8 and
count the jobs yourself.

287
00:20:07,227 --> 00:20:12,861
Next time: workflows 1 to 5, line by line.
Until then, look at the state first and

288
00:20:12,901 --> 00:20:15,420
type second. See you in the next one.
